. Abney & Thomas, contra,\ncite: Rule as to negligence by railroad: 20 S. C., 249; 19 Am. & Eng. R. C., 466; 20 S. C., 259; 50 S. C., 49; 23 S. C., 531; 19 S. C., 20; 34 S. C., 292; 35 S. C., 493. The person causing mental or physical disease, either indirectly or directly, from fright or nervous shock occasioned by his acts, .is responsible therefor: 151 N. \u00a5., 107; 31 Am. R. R., 589; 4 A. & E. R. Cas., 182; 79 Ala., 328; 73 Wis., 147; 73 la., 241; article by Algernon Sydney Morton on damages for consequences of fright caused by negligence, in The University Raw Review, March, 1897. Stock being killed by railroad presumes negligence: 26 S. C., 49.\nApril 19, 1898."}, "file_name": "0323-01", "first_page_order": 331, "last_page_order": 352}